AUDIO: 'Uh, quick question. Do you know how many AI models are out there right now? Well, according to HuggingFace, there are over 3 million publicly available models out there. And let\'s say you chose a model that you really like. You go to their'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'HuggingFace page. You try to download it, and there\'s like nine different quantizations, four different parameter sizes, and no indication on whether any of that will actually run on your'

[00:24] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: llmfit terminal UI showing a list of models with scores, tokens/second, quantization, disk, memory, context, and fit.
AUDIO: 'particular machine. Ideally, you would want to know if this model is going to throw an outofmemory error on you before you download the full 8 gigabytes. So, how do you decide which one to use? Well, luckily there is a tool for that'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now. It\'s called LLM fit and it looks at your actual hardware, your GPU, your RAM, your CPU, and then it tells you out of hundreds of models which ones will genuinely run well on your machine and'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how well will they perform. And as you can see here, I got a bunch of different devices on the table. So, in this video, we\'re going to take a look at LLM Fit, see how it works, and then I\'m going to test it out on a bunch of these machines'

[01:02] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: llmfit terminal UI on a Mac, showing system information (Apple M2 Max, 25.8 GB GPU avail) and a list of models.
AUDIO: 'and see which models fit each of them. It\'s going to be a lot of fun. So, let\'s dive into it. Now, at its core, LLM fit is just a simple terminal tool. You run one'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'command and then it detects your system, your CPU cores, RAM and GPU, including multi-GPU setups and then it scores your setup against a bunch of available models from their database. And not only'

[01:28] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator speaking to the camera, introducing the problem of choosing AI models.
AUDIO: 'that, but it also gives you an indication how many tokens per second you can expect on your local setup when running this particular model. And of course to properly estimate your hardware capabilities, it uses the best'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tools for the job depending on your GPU cards and your infrastructure. Plus, it also evaluates if you have any acceleration backend installed on your machine. And it also tries to estimate'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the best quantization level of that particular model based on your hardware requirements. And each model is scored against four criteria, quality, speed, fit, and context. and then those get'

[02:04] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Creator explaining how llmfit determines speed numbers.
AUDIO: 'combined into a composite score. But the way they are weighted against each other isn\'t always fixed. So for example, chat cares more about speed while reasoning weighs quality much higher. So the same'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model can rank differently depending on what you\'re actually trying to do with it. But how does LLM fit actually determine those speed numbers? Well, the way it works is that every token means reading the entire model\'s weights off'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your VRAMm once. So speed really comes down to how fast your VRAMm can feed the GPU. LLM fit identifies the real memory bandwidth from your specific card. So it takes that number and then runs it'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'against the model size to estimate how many tokens per second you\'ll actually get. And if you want to get into the weeds of all that, there\'s also an advanced config panel where you can tune all of that yourself. And they added'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that after someone on GitHub pointed out that token per second estimates were coming in too high for certain models. And if you have some other GPU that is not in the bandwidth table, it falls back to per backend constants. CUDA,'

[03:07] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: llmfit terminal UI showing a "Community Leaderboard" with simulated results for an Apple M1 Max.
AUDIO: 'Metal, Rockm, and so on. So you\'re never left with a zero estimate. You might just get a slightly less precise one. And one more cool thing is that they have a community leaderboard where you'

